source @arxiv/cybernetics/beer-1972
source @arxiv/cybernetics/beer-1979
source @arxiv/cybernetics/beer-1984
source @arxiv/cybernetics/conant-ashby-1970
source @arxiv/cybernetics/ashby-1952

prism @epistemologic/cybernetic/viable {
  focus viable
  project viable
  split viable
  shift viable
  settle viable
}

# === The identity carrier ===
#
# T_reg per spec §3.1 Read A. The substrate-altitude carrier for
# *what the system IS* — the S5 policy reading at altitude α+1.
# Routes through eigenform's identity-as-fixed-point recognition
# at the foundation altitude (property #9 of the cybernetic
# canon), but is declared HERE as a typed carrier at the viable
# altitude so consumer shards can name viable's T_reg without
# pulling the floor's fixed-point machinery.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the underlying ref. Two
# identities with byte-equal underlying references ARE the same
# identity under the substrate's content-addressing. Same shape
# as second_order.observer and distinction's mark carrier.
type identity = ref

# === The stability carrier ===
#
# T_regd per spec §3.1 Read A. The substrate-altitude carrier for
# *whether the system maintains identity under disturbance* — the
# S1-S4 operational reading at altitude α+1. The verdict surfaces
# against the substrate's existing `transparency<identity>` family
# per spec §3.1: success means the system's identity survives the
# disturbance; partial(opacity_map) means it survives with
# recoverable loss; failure(opacity_map) means it breaks.
#
# Per [[feedback-no-bare-types]] stability is a typed carrier with
# the identity it's stability-of recorded alongside the verdict;
# a consumer collecting stability readings can rank without losing
# identity-of-which-the-stability-is-being-read.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on (subject_identity, verdict).
type stability = {
  subject_identity: identity,
  verdict:          transparency(identity),
}

# === The algedonic-signal carrier ===
#
# Beer 1979 ch. 6. An S1→S5 emergency bypass: if an S1 detects a
# threat to system identity that the normal channels would damp
# out, the algedonic channel fires directly to S5. Declared HERE
# at viable's altitude as the typed carrier; the operational
# sibling at @cyberpunk/algedonic (per spec §10 item 6) carries
# the firing mechanism. Two species; one carrier.
#
# - `source`   the S1 raising the signal (the operational unit
#              detecting the identity threat).
# - `severity` the urgency reading; consumer-defined ordering on
#              the underlying ref. The substrate names the carrier;
#              the realisation closes the ordering per species.
# - `threat`   the identity-threat the S1 is reporting; a ref the
#              consumer interprets at the species boundary.
type algedonic_signal = {
  source:   ref,
  severity: ref,
  threat:   identity,
}

# === The viable_system carrier ===
#
# The substrate-altitude lift of the VSM S1-S5 structure as a
# typed record. Each field carries the species-specific data at
# the viable altitude:
#
# - `s5_policy`     the system's identity-policy; T_reg for the
#                   homeostat reading (Read A). Routes through
#                   eigenform's identity-as-fixed-point at the
#                   foundation altitude per cybernetic property #9.
# - `s4_intelligence` the model of the OUTSIDE the system inhabits;
#                   the elsewhere-and-elsewhen channel; carries the
#                   prediction surface per [[architecture-prediction-
#                   paradigm-orthogonal-to-optimization]].
# - `s3_audit`      the here-and-now management channel; sees the
#                   operational reality of the S1s. The S3-S4
#                   oscillation IS the regulation per spec §3.3.
# - `s2_coordination` the anti-oscillation between S1s; damps
#                   conflict via shared protocol.
# - `s1_operations` the variety producers; the parts that DO things.
#                   Each S1 is itself a viable system (Read B
#                   recursive viability per spec §3.2). Carried as
#                   a ref-list so the recursion descends without
#                   substrate re-declaration; the realisation closes
#                   the per-S1 viable carrier per consumer.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on (s5, s4, s3, s2, s1). Two
# viable_system records ARE the same viable system under the
# substrate's content-addressing iff all five subsystems are
# byte-equal. Same shape discipline as
# coherence-parametric.lock_pair.
type viable_system = {
  s5_policy:        identity,
  s4_intelligence:  ref,
  s3_audit:         ref,
  s2_coordination:  ref,
  s1_operations:    ref,
}

# === stability_of — the natural substitution τ : Identity ↔ Stability ===
#
# Spec §3.1 Read A: the substitution is natural because Ashby's
# homeostat law (Ashby 1952) says: the system's stability under
# disturbance IS its identity surviving the disturbance. Read off
# by *running the system* through a disturbance.
#
# `stability_of(i)` is the τ-action: given identity `i`, returns
# the stability reading at the species' altitude. The
# substitution's naturality is substrate-evident because the
# Identity carrier routes through eigenform (property #9: identity
# IS the recursion's fixed point); stability IS the verdict that
# this fixed-point survives one tick of disturbance.
#
# Body discharges per-species at the realisation boundary. Same
# shape as the parametric carrier's measurement actions and the
# four metalogue shims at `shards/code/metalogue.mirror`: the
# substrate declares the obligation; species bodies discharge when
# a consumer pulls.
stability_of(i: identity) -> stability { \ }

# === temporal_substitution — Read D's τ : Identity_t ↔ Identity_{t+Δt} ===
#
# Spec §3.4 Read D: the temporal-preservation lock. The system
# was-itself at time t; the system is-itself at time t+Δt; the
# substitution IS the identity-on-shape modulo time-evolution via
# the system's regulation.
#
# `temporal_substitution(i, dt_window)` is the Δt-window-parameterized
# τ-action: given identity `i` and the temporal window `dt_window`,
# returns the identity AFTER the window has elapsed (with the
# system's regulation having weathered whatever disturbance occurred
# during the window).
#
# This action IS the load-bearing surface for Read D's non-trivial
# ω: the dt_window IS what the parametric carrier's
# `connection_form { is_trivial: false, data: dt_window }` carries
# as data. Coevolution carries Kauffman NK lattice data in the same
# slot; viable carries the temporal-identity Δt-window. Same
# substrate-altitude slot, different species' interpretation.
#
# Body discharges per-species at the realisation boundary. The
# discharge IS the parametric carrier's lock_verdict applied with
# the species-specific ω data.
temporal_substitution(i: identity, dt_window: ref) -> identity { \ }

# === viability_law — Beer's necessary-and-sufficient condition ===
#
# Beer 1972 ch. 9, sharpened in Beer 1984 §3:
#
#   A system is viable if and only if it can maintain a separate
#   existence in the kind of environment it has to face.
#
# `viability_law(v)` reads whether the viable_system carrier `v`
# satisfies the necessary-and-sufficient condition: the
# regulation succeeds at preserving identity through time as the
# environment changes. The verdict IS the species' instantiation
# of the parametric lock_verdict — composing the five cybernetic-
# ancestor measurements at viable's altitude with the contraction-
# mode classification (PL fixed-point convergence for the static
# Read A; Red Queen bounded-sustainment for the temporal Read D
# per spec §3.4 and the parametric carrier's §8.10 extension).
#
# Body discharges `\` per [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]]; the
# discharge IS the composite verdict at the species boundary.
viability_law(v: viable_system) -> verdict { \ }

# === algedonic_fires — the S1→S5 bypass operation ===
#
# Beer 1979 ch. 6. When an S1 detects a threat to system identity
# that the normal channels would damp out, the algedonic channel
# fires directly to S5. Declared at viable's altitude so consumer
# shards can declare `algedonic_fires(signal, system)` without
# pulling the operational sibling at @cyberpunk/algedonic.
#
# The verdict reads whether the firing actually preserves
# viability (i.e. whether S5 receives the signal in time to
# adjust policy before the threat closes on the system's identity).
# `success` means the S5 has actionable time; `partial` means
# the firing arrived with degraded options; `failure` means the
# firing arrived too late to preserve identity (the system has
# crossed the viability boundary).
#
# Body discharges per-species at the realisation boundary.
algedonic_fires(signal: algedonic_signal, v: viable_system) -> verdict { \ }

# === viable_well_formed — the bilateral inheritance predicate ===
#
# `viable_well_formed(v)` is the predicate consumer shards name in
# a `requires` clause to declare bilateral agreement (per
# recognition #37, the Pask-agreement reading of `requires`) that
# the viable_system carrier `v` they hold IS a well-formed Beer
# viable system — i.e. the five subsystems are present at their
# declared altitudes; the algedonic channel exists; the viability
# law holds at the species boundary.
#
# The bilateral commitment: the carrier commits to declaring the
# viable_system five-tuple; the consumer commits to discharging
# the viability_law verdict at the species boundary.
#
# Declared as a typed predicate so downstream species shards can
# write `requires viable_well_formed(my_system)` — same shape as
# the parametric carrier's `parametric_lock_witnessing(my_lock)`,
# `variety.variety_preserving(species)`,
# `second_order.second_order_well_formed(carrier)`, and the
# metalogue `requires substrate_pull_preserving(shim_X)` pattern at
# `shards/code/metalogue.mirror`. Body is `\`; the discharge IS
# the conjunction of viability_law + algedonic_fires-well-typed
# at the species boundary.
#
# No `requires viable_well_formed(…)` clauses are added at THIS
# shard. The property declares the predicate; consumers add the
# clauses when they pull. Per [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]] the
# operational sibling at `@mirror/cybernetics/viable` lands the
# same way.
viable_well_formed(v: viable_system) -> verdict { \ }

out @epistemologic/cybernetic/viable
out identity
out stability
out algedonic_signal
out viable_system
out stability_of
out temporal_substitution
out viability_law
out algedonic_fires
out viable_well_formed
